    {
      "role": "user",
      "content": "Hey, I'm looking into some issues with a recent asphalt overlay we did here in Phoenix. We're seeing some premature longitudinal cracking in a specific section. Can you give me a technical summary of the primary causes for this kind of pavement failure, specifically looking at how thermal expansion and bitumen viscosity play into it?",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "Premature longitudinal cracking in asphalt, especially in desert climates like Phoenix, is often related to thermal stresses exceeding the asphalt's tensile strength. High diurnal temperature swings cause significant thermal expansion and contraction in the pavement layers. When the bitumen's viscosity is too high or the mix design is too stiff, it cannot accommodate these cyclical strains, leading to cracking parallel to the road direction. This results in stress concentration and ultimately pavement failure.",
      "source": "target",
