    {
      "role": "user",
      "content": "That makes sense, especially with the heat. I'm also looking at the subgrade data. Could you expand on how subgrade compaction or any potential moisture issues in the base layers might exacerbate those thermal stresses and contribute to the cracking?",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "Poor subgrade compaction or moisture issues severely reduce the underlying support stiffness, effectively lowering the pavement's overall structural capacity. When the base or subgrade softens due to moisture, it develops greater differential settlement, which introduces additional tensile strain onto the overlay even under thermal loading. This reduced support allows the pavement to deform more easily under thermal expansion/contraction forces, thus exacerbating existing stress weaknesses and accelerating longitudinal cracking.",
      "source": "target",
